<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Seleucia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Seleucia-on-Tigris</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Seleucia, or Seleucia-on-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>, was a city founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I Nicator</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> around the end of the fourth century BCE. It served as one of the twin capitals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> and became one of the largest cities of the Hellenistic world. It later passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a>, as accounts of the Seleucid Empire note in discussing the loss of Mesopotamia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seleucia.jpg" alt="Seleucia" width="296" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Renato de carvalho ferreira · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ctesiphon_map-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Urban centres such as <b>Seleucia</b> on the Tigris, with its large Greek population, and the neighbouring royal residence of Ctesiphon served as commercial and administrative hubs, and the silver drachms struck by the Arsacid kings, inscribed in Greek, reflected the blend of Iranian and Hellenistic influences that shaped…</blockquote><a href="/empire/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…Euphrates, whose ancient irrigation network could generate surpluses sufficient to sustain both dense urban populations and substantial armies; <b>Seleucia-on-Tigris</b>, founded by Seleucus I on the Tigris north of Babylon, grew into one of the largest cities of the Hellenistic world, with a population that ancient sources put in…</blockquote><a href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
